[97.832019, "o", "Fixed. Here's what was actually wrong and what I did.\r\n\r\n## Root cause\r\n\r\nNot a PATH problem or a broken `pip` script — your `/usr/local/bin/pip` was fine. The **importable `pip` package directory had been deleted** from site-packages while its metadata was left behind:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/\r\n├── README.txt\r\n└── pip-24.3.1.dist-info   ← metadata present, claimed pip was installed\r\n```\r\n\r\n`pip` therefore crashed at startup on `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` → `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\nTwo things compounded it:\r\n- The **orphaned `dist-info` made `ensurepip` refuse to repair**, reporting `Requirement already satisfied` while the code was still absent.\r\n- Nothing else blocked you: no `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker for 3.13, site-packages writable, 221 GB free.\r\n\r\nDirectory timestamps suggest the `pip/` folder was removed around **Oct 31 13:45** (parent dir modified then) while the Jan 2025 metadata survived — typical of an interrupted up"]
[97.832051, "o", "grade or a partial delete.\r\n\r\n## Fix\r\n\r\n1. Backed up the orphaned metadata to `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.bak`.\r\n2. Moved it aside to `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphan`.\r\n3. Re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip`, which installed pip from the bundled `pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` — **no network required**.\r\n\r\n## Verified\r\n\r\n| Check | Result |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| `pip --version` / `pip3 --version` | `pip 24.3.1 ... (python 3.13)` |\r\n| `python3 -m pip --version` | works |\r\n| `import pip` | resolves to `.../site-packages/pip/__init__.py` |\r\n| `pip check` | No broken requirements found |\r\n| PyPI index access | reachable (`six` 1.17.0 listed) |\r\n| Real install | `Successfully installed six-1.17.0` |\r\n| Uninstall | `Successfully uninstalled six-1.17.0` |\r\n\r\nI uninstalled the test package afterward, so your environment is left as I found it apart from the repair.\r\n\r\n## Two things worth knowing\r\n\r\n- **`/usr/bin/python3` (Debian's 3.11) has no pip at all** — a separate install from the one you were hitting"]
[97.832059, "o", ". If you need it, that's `apt install python3-pip`, not `ensurepip`; note it carries an `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker, so it resists system-wide installs by design. The `python3`/`pip` on your `PATH` is the 3.13 one I just repaired.\r\n- You're running as **root**, which is why every command printed pip's \"Running pip as the 'root' user\" warning. For actual project work prefer a venv (`python3 -m venv .venv`) to avoid this class of damage recurring — but system-wide installs will work now.\r\n\r\nOnce you're happy, you can delete the two backups: `rm -rf /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.bak /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.orphan`. I left them in place in case you want to inspect them.\r\n"]
